    "uid": "24fe1568f7f50ecefcec954f94c770e2",
    "title": "Shell history tampering",
    "desc": "A program wiped, truncated or redirected a shell history file - rm / truncate / shred of ~/.bash_history (and zsh/sh equivalents), or symlinking it to /dev/null so nothing is ever recorded. Clearing command history is how an intruder covers their tracks after the fact (MITRE T1070.003). Honest limit: this catches history tampering done by a *program*; the shell-builtin forms (history -c, unset HISTFILE, set +o history) run no process and so are invisible to any exec-based detector - banken sees programs, not builtins. Disabling history by editing a shell rc is caught separately by the persistence rule (shell_rc).",
